+---
+title: Parsing user strings in ParamType.convert (click.Duration)
+author: HarzerHeribert
+date: 2026-10-16
+cost: { minutes: 3, tokens: 55399 }
+touches:
+  - CHANGES.md
+  - docs/api.md
+  - docs/parameter-types.md
+  - src/click/__init__.py
+  - src/click/types.py
+  - tests/test_types.py
+---
+
+## Decided
+- click.Duration (src/click/types.py) parses with a compiled regex and `re.fullmatch`, flags `re.ASCII | re.IGNORECASE`. A bare whole number is turned into "<n>s" and parsed with the same regex.
+- Anything that isn't a str or a timedelta (including int defaults such as `default=120`) fails, because the spec says so. Use `default="120"` or a timedelta.
+- Failures go through `self.fail(_("{value!r} is not a valid duration."))`, matching UUIDParameterType.
+
+## Tried and ruled out
+- `re.match` with `$`: `$` matches before a trailing newline, so "120\n" would be accepted.
+- `str.isdigit()` or `\d` without re.ASCII: these accept non-ASCII digits ("١٢", and superscripts that `int()` rejects).
+- re.IGNORECASE without re.ASCII: "ſ" (U+017F) case-folds to "s", so "45ſ" would parse.
+
+## Watch out
+- `timedelta(...)` raises OverflowError beyond 999999999 days, and `int()` raises ValueError past roughly 4300 digits. Catch both and call self.fail, or users get a traceback instead of a usage error.
+- New types need four places updated: types.py, the alphabetical export list in __init__.py, docs/api.md, and docs/parameter-types.md (listing plus example). Add a CHANGES.md entry as well.
